   "source": [
    "## Step 2: Why chunk?\n",
    "\n",
    "Why not put the **whole** `leave_policy.txt` in Chroma as one item?\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **One vector for many ideas is blurry.** The file talks about paid leave, how to ask for leave, and sick leave. Its one vector is a **mix**, so a question about sick days does not match it well.\n",
    "2. **Too many tokens.** A question about sick days does not need the whole file. We want to send only the part that helps (Day 24: tokens = cost).\n",
    "\n",
    "But a chunk must not be **too small** either. *\"It is 10 days.\"* What is 10 days? The meaning is lost.\n",
    "\n",
    "*Everyday example:* a student asks one question about a textbook. You do not photocopy the **whole book**, and you do not cut out **one word**. You copy the **one paragraph** that answers it.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Goal:** one chunk = **one idea**, complete enough to read alone."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Step 3: The ways to chunk\n",
    "\n",
    "There are many ways to cut a document. Here is the full list. We try the first ones in code and explain the rest with small examples.\n",
    "\n",
    "| # | Way | How it cuts | Cost |\n",
    "|---|---|---|---|\n",
    "| 1 | **Fixed-size** | Every N characters or tokens | Very cheap |\n",
    "| 2 | **Sliding window** | Fixed-size, but chunks **overlap** | Cheap |\n",
    "| 3 | **Sentence-based** | At the end of each sentence | Cheap |\n",
    "| 4 | **Paragraph-based** | At each blank line | Cheap |\n",
    "| 5 | **Recursive** | Try big cuts first, then smaller cuts, until it fits | Cheap |\n",
    "| 6 | **Semantic** | Where the **meaning** changes (uses embeddings) | Medium |\n",
    "| 7 | **Hierarchical** | Parent (section) and child (paragraph) chunks | Medium |\n",
    "| 8 | **Contextual** | Add heading, source or date to each chunk | Cheap |\n",
    "| 9 | **Topic-based** | Group text by topic | Medium |\n",
    "| 10 | **Modality-specific** | A different way for text, tables and images | Medium |\n",
    "| 11 | **AI-driven** | An LLM chooses where to cut | Expensive |\n",
    "| 12 | **Agentic** | An AI agent picks a way for each part of the document | Very expensive |"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Way 1: Fixed-size chunking\n",
    "\n",
    "**Concept:** cut every N characters (or words, or tokens). It does not look at the meaning.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Good:** very fast and simple. **Bad:** it cuts words and sentences in the middle.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Use it for:** a quick first try, or big piles of plain text where speed matters more than quality (like old log files)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
