    "### Way 9: Topic-based chunking\n",
    "\n",
    "**Concept:** group the text by **topic**. A long text can jump between topics. We put all sentences of one topic in one chunk. Tools for this: topic models (like LDA) or clustering the embeddings.\n",
    "\n",
    "*Small example:* a meeting note has these sentences:\n",
    "\n",
    "| Sentence | Topic |\n",
    "|---|---|\n",
    "| \"The budget is Rs 5 million.\" | money |\n",
    "| \"We hire 3 new engineers.\" | people |\n",
    "| \"Cost must stay below Rs 5 million.\" | money |\n",
    "\n",
    "Topic-based chunking gives **2 chunks**: one for *money* (sentences 1 and 3), one for *people* (sentence 2). Semantic chunking only cuts when the topic changes, so it would give 3 chunks.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Use it for:** long reports or articles that move between subjects."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**✏️ Quick Check:** What is the difference between semantic and topic-based chunking?"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Answer:** semantic chunking cuts when the topic **changes** (neighbours only). Topic-based chunking **groups** all sentences of the same topic, even if they are far apart."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Way 10: Modality-specific chunking\n",
    "\n",
    "**Concept:** a document may have **text, tables and images**. Each one needs its **own** way of cutting.\n",
    "\n",
    "| Content | How to chunk |\n",
    "|---|---|\n",
    "| Text | By sentence or by paragraph |\n",
    "| Table | One chunk **per row**, with the column names added |\n",
    "| Image | Use the caption (or a description) as the text |\n",
    "\n",
    "If we cut a table like normal text, a row like `Laptop | 150000` loses its column names. We must add them back.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Use it for:** PDFs and web pages that mix text, tables and images, like a bank statement or a product page."
   ]
